        "heading": "Raw vs Parsed, Structured vs Unstructured",
        "content": "When a system first writes a log, it writes it in whatever format its designers chose. That original, untouched form is called the **raw log**. Raw logs are honest but messy: some are neat, some are a wall of text, and every product does it a little differently. Before that raw data becomes easy to search, something usually cleans it up — and understanding that journey keeps you from being confused when the same event looks different in two places.\n\n### Structured vs unstructured\n\nA **structured** log already separates its data into clear labeled fields. The most common structured format you will meet is **JSON**, which wraps each value in an obvious \"name\": value pair, like {\"user\":\"jsmith\",\"action\":\"login\"}. You can hand JSON to a computer and it knows exactly where the username is. Structured logs are a gift — the work of splitting the event into fields is already done.\n\nAn **unstructured** log is free-form text: a human-readable sentence with no clear boundaries between values. \"User jsmith logged in from 10.4.2.19 at 8:14\" is unstructured. A person can read it, but a computer has to be *taught* where the username ends and the address begins. Much of the older logging in the world is unstructured or semi-structured, which is why the next idea exists.\n\n### Key-value pairs and normalization\n\nBetween fully structured and fully unstructured sits the **key-value pair** style, which you saw in the earlier examples: user.name=jsmith source.ip=10.4.2.19. Each field is a key, an equals sign, and a value. It is not as rigid as JSON but far easier to read than a plain sentence, and it is extremely common.\n\nHere is the crucial part for a SOC analyst. Every product names its fields differently. One firewall calls the source address \"src\"; another calls it \"source_ip\"; a cloud log calls it \"callerIpAddress.\" If you had to remember every product's private vocabulary, investigating would be impossible. So a **SIEM** (Security Information and Event Management system — the central platform where all your logs are collected and searched) performs **normalization**, also called **parsing**: it takes each raw log, figures out what every value means, and rewrites it into one shared set of field names. After normalization, that firewall's \"src\" and the cloud's \"callerIpAddress\" both become the same field — often source.ip — so you can search across every source at once.\n\n### Why you still need the raw log\n\nParsed logs are easier, but never throw away your respect for the raw version. Parsing can go wrong — a field lands in the wrong place, or a value gets dropped. When something looks strange, a seasoned analyst pulls up the original raw log to confirm what the machine actually wrote before drawing conclusions. The parsed view is the convenient summary; the raw log is the ground truth. Knowing both, and knowing when to distrust the tidy version, is a mark of an analyst who has been burned once and learned. Always ask: is this field what the machine reported, or what the parser decided?",
        "codeExample": "// The SAME login event in three formats. Learn to recognize all three.\n\n// 1) UNSTRUCTURED (free text) — readable by a human, painful for a machine\nJul  4 08:14:22 FIN-WKS-07 User jsmith logged in from 10.4.2.19 (success)\n\n// 2) KEY-VALUE (semi-structured) — each field is key=value, easy to scan\ntime=2026-07-04T08:14:22Z host=FIN-WKS-07 user=jsmith src=10.4.2.19 outcome=success\n\n// 3) STRUCTURED (JSON) — fully labeled, ready for a computer to index\n{ \"@timestamp\": \"2026-07-04T08:14:22Z\", \"host\": \"FIN-WKS-07\",\n  \"user\": { \"name\": \"jsmith\" }, \"source\": { \"ip\": \"10.4.2.19\" },\n  \"event\": { \"action\": \"user_login\", \"outcome\": \"success\" } }\n\n// After the SIEM NORMALIZES all three, they share one vocabulary:\n// user.name=jsmith   source.ip=10.4.2.19   event.action=user_login   event.outcome=success",
