        "heading": "How to Read an Unfamiliar Log",
        "content": "You will constantly face logs from products you have never used. The beginner panics; the trained analyst stays calm, because the method for reading a strange log is always the same. You are not memorizing every product — you are applying a repeatable process that works everywhere.\n\n### Step one: find the anchors\n\nStart exactly where you started in the first section: locate the timestamp (when), the source identity (who — a user, a host, an IP), and the action (what). These three appear in essentially every log, and finding them orients you instantly. Scan for anything that looks like a date or time. Scan for anything that looks like a username or a computer name or an IP address. Scan for a verb — login, create, delete, connect, block, allow. In under a minute you have the skeleton of the event even in a log you have never seen.\n\n### Step two: use field-name intuition\n\nMany modern logs, especially after a SIEM normalizes them, follow naming conventions that you can *predict* once you know the pattern. Fields are often grouped by a category prefix followed by a dot and a specific attribute. Learn these families and you can guess field meanings on sight:\n\n**source.*** and **destination.*** describe the two ends of a network connection: source.ip, source.port, destination.ip, destination.port. Source is where something came from; destination is where it went. **user.*** describes the human or account: user.name, user.domain, user.id. **host.*** or **device.*** describes the machine: host.name, host.os. **event.*** describes the action itself: event.action (the verb — what happened), event.outcome (success or failure), event.category (the general type). **process.*** describes a running program: process.name, process.pid (the process ID number), process.command_line (the exact command that was run).\n\nOnce these families live in your head, an unfamiliar log stops being intimidating. You see \"event.action\" and you know that is the verb. You see \"source.ip\" and you know where it came from. You do not need documentation for the common cases — you have intuition.\n\n### Step three: read the who / what / when as a sentence\n\nThe final move is to assemble the anchors into a plain-English sentence and say it out loud (or in your head): \"At 8:14 UTC, user jsmith on host FIN-WKS-07 successfully logged in from 10.4.2.19.\" If you can produce that sentence, you have read the log. Everything after that is deciding whether the sentence is *normal* or *worth worrying about* — which is the subject of the next section.\n\n### When a field is genuinely unknown\n\nSometimes a field truly is product-specific and you cannot guess it. That is fine. Note it, and if it seems relevant, look it up in the vendor's field reference rather than guessing — a wrong guess about a field's meaning is how analysts reach confident but false conclusions. The discipline is knowing the difference between a field you can safely infer and one you must verify. Guess the common families; verify the exotic ones. That balance is what lets you move fast without fooling yourself.",
        "codeExample": "// An UNFAMILIAR log line from a product you've never seen. Read it with the method.\ntimeGenerated=2026-07-04T09:02:41Z DeviceName=SALES-LT-14 InitiatingProcessFileName=mshta.exe \\\n  InitiatingProcessAccountName=akhan RemoteUrl=hxxp://cdn-updates[.]info/x.hta ActionType=NetworkConnection\n\n// STEP 1 - Anchors:\n//   WHEN -> timeGenerated=2026-07-04T09:02:41Z\n//   WHO  -> DeviceName=SALES-LT-14, InitiatingProcessAccountName=akhan\n//   WHAT -> ActionType=NetworkConnection, InitiatingProcessFileName=mshta.exe\n\n// STEP 2 - Field-name intuition (even with vendor-specific names):\n//   \"DeviceName\"          ~ host.name\n//   \"InitiatingProcess*\"  ~ process.* (the program that acted)\n//   \"RemoteUrl\"           ~ destination / url\n\n// STEP 3 - Say it as a sentence:\n//   \"At 09:02 UTC, on SALES-LT-14, account akhan's mshta.exe made a network\n//    connection to cdn-updates[.]info.\" -> You just read a log you'd never seen."
