        "heading": "Timestamps, Time Zones, and Why the Clock Lies",
        "content": "The most important field in almost any log is also the one beginners trust most naively: the **timestamp**. An investigation is fundamentally about *sequence* — what happened, then what happened next — and the entire sequence collapses if you misread the clock. Time is where new analysts make their most consequential mistakes, so it deserves its own treatment.\n\nThe core trap is **time zones**. Different systems log in different zones: a firewall might record in **UTC**, a Windows workstation in the local zone (say, US Eastern), a cloud service in UTC again. If you line up their raw timestamps and sort them, you get a *false* order — a workstation event stamped `09:03` local looks five hours *earlier* than a firewall event stamped `14:03` UTC, even though they happened at the same instant. Conclude from that raw view and you will wrongly decide the workstation activity \"wasn't part of this incident.\" The fix is a single unbreakable habit: **convert every timestamp to UTC before you correlate.** UTC is the analyst's universal clock precisely because it has no daylight-saving shifts and no local offset — it is the common ground where events from every source can be truthfully ordered.\n\nSeveral related time problems recur, and knowing them keeps you from being fooled:\n\n- **Clock skew.** A host whose clock is wrong misplaces its own events by however much it drifts — minutes or hours. Organisations run **NTP** (Network Time Protocol — the standard system that keeps computer clocks synchronised to a shared reference time) to keep clocks synchronised for exactly this reason, and a host with obvious drift is both a correlation hazard and a data-quality finding.\n- **Event time vs ingest time.** The moment an event *happened* is not the moment the SIEM *received* it. A log delayed in transit arrives late but is not a late attack; confusing the two makes you misjudge timelines and dwell time.\n- **Ambiguous or missing zones.** A timestamp with no zone is meaningless — \"09:03\" is not a time until you know 09:03 *where*. Always know which zone a field is in before you reason from it.\n\nThe discipline to carry away is simple and non-negotiable: **anchor your timeline in UTC, know which clock each source used, and account for skew and delay.** Most \"the events don't line up\" confusion in real investigations is not a mystery about the attack — it is a mistake about time.",
        "image": {
          "src": "/lesson-images/logs/timestamps-utc-correlation.svg",
          "alt": "A diagram showing the same event recorded by three systems in different time zones. As recorded, they look out of order: the firewall (UTC) logs 14:03:11 block outbound, the workstation (EST) logs 09:03:09 process start, and the cloud app (UTC) logs 14:03:14 data export — so sorting by raw time makes the workstation event look five hours earlier and you would wrongly conclude it was not part of the incident. Converted to UTC, the truth emerges: 14:03:09 workstation process start (EST plus 5), 14:03:11 firewall block outbound, 14:03:14 cloud data export — a clear five-second chain, one incident. The rules that keep time honest: always convert to UTC before correlating; watch for clock skew (a wrong host clock misplaces events, NTP keeps clocks synced); know which time you are reading (event time versus ingest time); and record the zone, because a timestamp with no zone is ambiguous.",
          "caption": "The same event across three systems looks out of order until you convert to UTC — then the true five-second chain appears. Anchor every timeline in UTC.",
          "credit": "Figure authored for this course."
        }
