      {
        "heading": "When Logs Lie: Gaps, Tampering, and the Cleared Log",
        "content": "A dangerous unspoken assumption behind reading logs is that the logs are *complete and honest*. Attackers know you rely on them, so a routine part of a serious intrusion is attacking the logs themselves — and an analyst who treats a log's silence as proof of safety can be led straight past a breach. Learning to read the *absence* of logs is as important as reading their content.\n\nAttackers interfere with logging in a few recognisable ways:\n\n- **Clearing logs.** After acting, an attacker may wipe the record to cover their tracks. On Windows this is loud in its own right: **Event ID 1102 (the security audit log was cleared)** is a high-signal alert, because there is almost never a legitimate reason for it. On Linux, truncating or deleting `/var/log` files, or clearing shell history, is the equivalent. The clearing event is itself evidence — attackers cannot erase the *fact* that erasure happened, only its contents.\n- **Disabling or tampering with logging.** Stopping the logging service, changing audit policy to stop recording an action, or unhooking an EDR sensor produces a **gap** — a stretch where a source that is normally chatty goes quiet. This maps to **MITRE ATT&CK** (a public, industry-standard catalog of attacker techniques, each with an ID like T1562) — specifically **Impair Defenses** (T1562) and **Indicator Removal** (T1070).\n- **Log injection.** More rarely, an attacker inserts forged or misleading entries to send an investigation the wrong way.\n\nThe mental shift this demands is the one that separates a competent analyst from a checkbox reader: **a gap is a signal, not a blank.** If a server logs steadily every few seconds and then goes silent for twenty minutes during exactly the window you are investigating, that silence is not \"nothing happened\" — it is a lead. The right questions become: *why* did this source stop, was logging disabled, was the host offline, or is the gap the shape of the attacker's activity? Corroboration across sources is your defence here — if the workstation log is suspiciously quiet but the firewall and the domain controller still saw that host doing things, the workstation's silence is exposed as tampering rather than truth.\n\nThe takeaway reframes trust: logs are evidence, but evidence can be attacked. **Absence of logs is never automatically absence of activity** — and a cleared log, a disabled sensor, or an unexplained gap is often the loudest thing in the whole investigation."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Retention and Visibility: You Cannot Investigate What Was Never Kept",
        "content": "Every technique in this lesson assumes the log you need *exists* and is *still there*. Two organisational realities decide whether that assumption holds — **visibility** (was it ever collected?) and **retention** (is it still available?) — and understanding them explains why some investigations simply hit a wall, and how to turn that wall into a recommendation instead of a dead end.\n\n**Visibility is about coverage: you can only investigate what was logged in the first place.** If a system was never sending its logs to your SIEM — an unmonitored server, a SaaS app nobody onboarded, a network segment with no sensor — then no amount of skill recovers what was never recorded. This is the \"garbage in\" problem stated from the analyst's side: a blind spot is not something you can query around, and a large share of \"we couldn't determine what happened\" outcomes trace back to a source that was never collected. Recognising a visibility gap is itself a valuable finding, because it tells the organisation exactly where it is defenceless.\n\n**Retention is about time: logs are kept only for a finite window, then deleted.** Storage costs money, so organisations set retention periods — verbose sources like network flow might be kept for weeks, while high-value security logs are kept for months or a year or more, often driven by **compliance requirements** (regulations frequently mandate minimum retention, e.g. keeping security logs for a defined period). The consequence for investigations is stark: attacker **dwell time** — the gap between intrusion and detection — routinely runs into weeks or months, so if a breach began before your retention window, **the earliest and most important evidence may already have been deleted before you ever start looking.**\n\nFor the analyst, this turns two frustrations into professional actions:\n\n- **When you hit a missing source, name the gap.** \"I could not confirm the initial access because we do not collect logs from that VPN appliance\" is a finding that drives a real improvement — closing a visibility gap — not a failure.\n- **When evidence has aged out, say so and note the retention limit.** \"The activity likely began earlier, but our flow logs only retain 14 days\" tells responders the true uncertainty and makes the case for longer retention on critical sources.\n\nThe through-line for the whole log topic: reading a log line is a skill, but knowing *what was never captured and what has already expired* is the judgment that keeps you honest — because the most important log in an investigation is sometimes the one that was never collected, or the one that was deleted last Tuesday."
      },
      {
        "heading": "The Syslog Standard: Facility, Severity, and Standardized Formats (CEF/LEEF)",
        "content": "Everything in the previous sections assumed a log had already arrived somewhere sensible and readable. But logs have to travel — from the device that wrote them to the SIEM that stores them — and for decades the protocol that carries the overwhelming majority of that traffic, especially from network gear, Linux/Unix systems, and appliances, has been **syslog**. Understanding syslog is not optional trivia: it is the plumbing underneath a huge share of the log lines you will ever read, and its two core numbers — facility and severity — appear, one way or another, in almost every log format that came after it.\n\n### The syslog standard: RFC 5424\n\nSyslog began as an informal convention and was later formalized by the IETF as **RFC 5424** (\"The Syslog Protocol\"), which defines the structure of a syslog message and how it is transported. A syslog message is built from a **PRI** (priority) value, a **HEADER** (timestamp, hostname, application name), and a **MSG** (the actual content, which can be free text or structured data). The PRI value is what makes syslog searchable and filterable at scale, because it encodes two pieces of metadata as a single number.\n\n### Facility and severity: the two numbers behind PRI\n\nThe **facility** identifies *what kind of process or subsystem* generated the message — the kernel, a mail system, an authentication subsystem, a print spooler, or one of eight generic \"local use\" slots (local0 through local7) that organizations assign to their own applications. The **severity** identifies *how urgent* the message is, on an eight-level scale where, counterintuitively, **lower numbers mean more severe** — 0 is the worst, 7 is the least.\n\n| Severity | Level | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 0 | Emergency | System is unusable |\n| 1 | Alert | Action must be taken immediately |\n| 2 | Critical | Critical condition |\n| 3 | Error | Error condition |\n| 4 | Warning | Warning condition |\n| 5 | Notice | Normal but significant condition |\n| 6 | Informational | Informational message |\n| 7 | Debug | Debug-level message |\n\nThe PRI value packed into every syslog message is calculated with one formula: **PRI = facility x 8 + severity**. A message from facility 4 (security/authorization) at severity 2 (critical) yields a PRI of 34. Once you know the formula, a bare number like <34> at the start of a raw syslog line stops being a mystery and becomes two decoded facts: which subsystem, and how bad.\n\n### Why this matters for triage\n\nSeverity is one of the fastest, cheapest triage signals a SIEM can give you *before* you read a single word of the message body. A flood of severity-6 informational lines is noise you can safely skim; a severity-1 alert deserves your eyes immediately, regardless of source. Analysts who understand facility/severity can build searches like \"show me every severity 0-2 event from any host in the last hour\" across an entire fleet of otherwise unrelated devices — firewalls, Linux servers, printers — because they all speak the same eight-level vocabulary. This is exactly the kind of shared vocabulary the normalization concept from earlier in this lesson depends on, except here the standard existed before the SIEM ever touched the log.\n\n### Transport: how the message actually travels\n\nSyslog was originally sent over **UDP port 514** — fast, connectionless, and consequently unreliable: a dropped UDP packet is a permanently lost log line, with no retry and no error. Because security logs are exactly the data you cannot afford to lose, most serious deployments now use **TCP** for guaranteed delivery, and security-conscious ones use **TLS-encrypted syslog (RFC 5425)** so the log traffic itself cannot be intercepted or spoofed in transit. Recognizing which transport a source uses tells you something about its reliability: a UDP-only legacy appliance is a source where you should expect occasional silent gaps that are transport loss, not tampering — a distinction that matters given the earlier lesson section on logs that lie.\n\n### Standardized event formats built on top of syslog: CEF and LEEF\n\nSyslog defines the *envelope*, but it says very little about the *shape of the message* inside it — which is exactly the raw-vs-structured problem from earlier in this lesson. Two vendor-driven standards emerged to solve that specifically for security logs, so that products from different vendors could describe an event in one predictable shape.\n\n**CEF (Common Event Format)**, originally defined by ArcSight, wraps a pipe-delimited header around a syslog message: CEF:Version|Device Vendor|Device Product|Device Version|Signature ID|Name|Severity|Extension. The Extension is then a series of key=value pairs, similar in spirit to the key-value logs from earlier in this lesson. Because every vendor that emits CEF fills in the same seven header fields in the same order, a SIEM can reliably extract \"who made this\" and \"how severe\" from any CEF-formatted product without writing a custom parser for each one.\n\n**LEEF (Log Event Extended Format)**, IBM's equivalent standard built for QRadar, plays the identical role with a slightly different header shape (LEEF:Version|Vendor|Product|Version|EventID|key=value pairs). You will not need to memorize LEEF's exact syntax, but you should recognize it on sight as \"the IBM cousin of CEF\" — the same idea of a standardized, vendor-neutral event shape, from a different ecosystem.\n\nThe throughline across this whole section is the same one from the normalization discussion earlier: raw diversity is the natural state of logging, and every layer of the industry — from the eight severity levels in a 1980s RFC to CEF's pipe-delimited header — exists to fight that diversity back down to something an analyst can search in one place.",
        "codeExample": "// A) A raw syslog line, decoded piece by piece.\n<34>1 2026-07-04T08:14:22.003Z fw-edge-01 sshd 8421 - - Failed password for invalid user admin from 185.99.4.12 port 51422 ssh2\n\n// PRI <34> decodes as: facility * 8 + severity = 34\n//   34 / 8 = 4 remainder 2  ->  facility 4 (security/authorization), severity 2 (Critical)\n// HEADER: version=1, timestamp=2026-07-04T08:14:22.003Z, hostname=fw-edge-01, app=sshd, procid=8421\n// MSG: \"Failed password for invalid user admin from 185.99.4.12 port 51422 ssh2\"\n\n// B) The SAME kind of event, wrapped in CEF (Common Event Format) by a different vendor:\nCEF:0|OpenSSH|sshd|9.6|AUTH_FAIL|Failed password authentication|7|src=185.99.4.12 duser=admin spt=51422 dpt=22\n\n// CEF header, pipe-delimited, always in this order:\n//   Version=0 | Device Vendor=OpenSSH | Device Product=sshd | Device Version=9.6\n//   | Signature ID=AUTH_FAIL | Name=Failed password authentication | Severity=7\n// Extension (key=value, same idea as the key-value logs from earlier in this lesson):\n//   src=185.99.4.12  duser=admin  spt=51422  dpt=22\n\n// Two very different vendors, two different raw shapes — but because both follow a\n// STANDARD (syslog's facility/severity, CEF's pipe-delimited header), a SIEM can\n// reliably extract \"how severe\" and \"who reported it\" without a custom parser for each."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Windows Event Log Architecture: Channels, EVTX, and Centralized Collection (WEF)",
        "content": "The 4624 logon record examined earlier in this lesson had to live somewhere on the machine before any SIEM ever saw it. This section covers that \"somewhere\" — the Windows Event Log itself — and, just as important, how organizations get those records off of one laptop and into a central place where an attacker cannot quietly delete them.\n\n### Channels: Windows Logs vs Applications and Services Logs\n\nWindows organizes its native event records into named **channels**, and Event Viewer (the built-in graphical tool for browsing them) groups those channels into two broad categories. **Windows Logs** contains a small set of general-purpose channels present on every machine: **Application** (records from installed programs), **Security** (logons, privilege use, object access — the channel that holds Event ID 4624 and 4688 from earlier in this lesson), **System** (operating-system and driver events), **Setup** (installation activity), and **Forwarded Events** (a destination channel, covered below). **Applications and Services Logs** is a much larger, extensible category where individual components register their own dedicated channels — this is where **Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational** lives, holding every Sysmon event ID referenced elsewhere in this course, completely separate from the native Security channel.\n\n### EVTX: the file format underneath every channel\n\nSince Windows Vista, every channel is physically stored on disk as a binary **.evtx** file (the older, plain-text-adjacent .evt format predates it and is now obsolete). EVTX is a structured, XML-based binary format — each event is really an XML document under the hood, which is why exporting an event from Event Viewer as XML shows you the same fields you saw rendered as friendly text, just in raw form. Knowing that EVTX exists matters practically: forensic tools parse .evtx files directly from a disk image even when a machine is offline, and understanding that a \"cleared log\" (the Event ID 1102 scenario from earlier in this lesson) really means the .evtx file's contents were wiped — not that the concept of logging was somehow disabled — sharpens what that tampering actually did and did not achieve.\n\n### Getting logs off the host before an attacker can touch them: Windows Event Forwarding\n\nA log that only exists on the compromised machine is a log an administrator-level attacker can delete — which is precisely the \"when logs lie\" problem from earlier in this lesson. **Windows Event Forwarding (WEF)** is Microsoft's built-in, agent-free answer: source machines subscribe to forward specific channels (typically Security) to one or more central **Windows Event Collector (WEC)** servers, authenticated and encrypted using Kerberos (inside a domain) or TLS. Once an event has been forwarded, it lands in the **Forwarded Events** channel on the collector and, from there, typically flows into the SIEM — and critically, it now exists in a place the compromised endpoint's own attacker-level access cannot reach to delete.\n\nWEF subscriptions come in two shapes: **source-initiated**, where many endpoints are configured (often via Group Policy) to push events to a collector without the collector needing to know every hostname in advance, and **collector-initiated**, where the WEC server is configured with an explicit list of source machines to pull from. Delivery can use the **Events (Render)** mode, which sends the same human-readable text seen in Event Viewer, or the **Events** (binary XML) mode, which is more compact and closer to the raw EVTX structure.\n\n### Why this belongs in an analyst's mental model\n\nTwo practical habits fall directly out of this architecture. First, when a host's local Security log looks suspiciously thin or absent, check whether the *forwarded* copy on the WEC/SIEM side tells a different story — a gap that exists locally but not centrally is strong evidence of local tampering rather than genuine inactivity. Second, when you cannot explain a gap in what you can see, ask whether the host in question was ever *onboarded* to forwarding at all — an unforwarded machine is a visibility gap in exactly the sense the retention-and-visibility section of this lesson described, except the fix here has a name: extend the WEF subscription to that host.",
        "codeExample": "// A) Where a channel lives on disk (the file behind Event Viewer's \"Security\" node):\nC:\\Windows\\System32\\winevt\\Logs\\Security.evtx\nC:\\Windows\\System32\\winevt\\Logs\\Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon%4Operational.evtx\n\n// B) The SAME 4624 event, shown in Event Viewer's raw XML export -- this is the\n// literal structure stored inside the .evtx file, before Event Viewer renders it\n// as friendly text:\n<Event xmlns=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/win/2004/08/events/event\">\n  <System>\n    <Provider Name=\"Microsoft-Windows-Security-Auditing\" />\n    <EventID>4624</EventID>\n    <TimeCreated SystemTime=\"2026-07-04T08:14:22.003Z\" />\n    <Computer>FIN-WKS-07.corp.local</Computer>\n  </System>\n  <EventData>\n    <Data Name=\"TargetUserName\">jsmith</Data>\n    <Data Name=\"LogonType\">2</Data>\n    <Data Name=\"IpAddress\">10.4.2.19</Data>\n  </EventData>\n</Event>\n\n// C) Setting up Windows Event Forwarding so the Security channel leaves the host\n// BEFORE a local admin-level attacker could ever clear it:\nwecutil qc                                   // configure this machine as a WEC collector\nwecutil cs subscription.xml                  // create a subscription that pulls Security\n                                              //   events from a defined list of source hosts\n\n// Once forwarded, the event also lives in \"Forwarded Events\" on the collector --\n// so even if an attacker later runs Event ID 1102 (clear the local Security log)\n// on FIN-WKS-07, the copy already sitting on the WEC server / SIEM survives intact."
      }
